Exploring how simple structural alignment may give rise to higher-level concepts.

1.

I am not letting connections be created arbitrarily.
They are guided by the existing human vocabulary system.

---

2.

For example, the transition from 2D to 3D: I consider this a major abstraction for intelligence.
I imagine a child moving around a 3D object—up and down, left and right, front and back—observing it from different angles and obtaining a sequence of continuously changing images.
Through this process, the child forms an intuitive understanding of the 3D object.

I try to guide the model to simulate human vision by changing viewpoints, so that these connections gradually form.
This evolving trajectory of connections can itself be seen as a kind of image.

---

3.

For quantities, I do not introduce additional algorithms.
Instead, I provide the model with images: one apple, two apples, two toy cars, etc.
From these images, the concept of number emerges.

For example, the human eye first sees one apple and associates it with “apple.”
Then it sees another apple and again associates it with “apple.”
When seeing two apples together, how does the association work?

In the model, “seeing” is represented by connections.
Seeing two apples corresponds to two bundles of connections.
These two bundles of connections form a new image.
Similarly, there are images formed by two toy cars, two chairs, and so on.

Between these images, parallel structures appear, and from this, the number “2” is abstracted.

---

Finally:

These ideas are not difficult to implement using explicit algorithms.
The difficulty is to consistently use “parallel connections” as the sole driving mechanism.

Like in humans, I believe the underlying driver is the activity of neurons.

This note focuses on minimal mechanisms.
Broader cognitive interpretations (e.g. spatial learning, abstraction) are left for future work.
